putation. These protocols enable a computationally weak verifier to offload computations to a powerful but untrusted prover, while providing the verifier with a guarantee that the prover performed the computations correctly. Despite substantial progress, existing implementations are not yet practical.\nL17: The main bottleneck is typically the extra effort required by the prover to return an answer with a guarantee of correctness, compared to returning an answer with no guarantee.\nL18: > We describe a refinement of a powerful interactive proof protocol originally due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum. Cormode, Mitzenmacher, and Thaler show how to implement the prover in this protocol in time O(S log S), where S is the size of an arithmetic circuit computing the function of interest. Our refinements apply to circuits whose wiring pattern is sufficiently \"regular\"; for these circuits, we bring the runtime of the prover down to O(S).\nL19: That is, our prover can evaluate the circuit with a guarantee of correctness, with only a constant-factor blowup in work compared to evaluating the circuit with no guarantee.\nL20: > We argue that our refinements capture a large class of circuits, and prove some theorems formalizing this. Experimentally, our refinements yield a 200x speedup for the prover over the implementation of Cormode et al., and our prover is less than 10x slower than a C++ program that simply evaluates the circuit. Along the way, we describe a special-purpose protocol for matrix multiplication that is of interest in its own right.\nL21: > Our final contribution is a protocol targeted at general data parallel computation. Compared to prior work, this protocol can more efficiently verify complicated computations as long as that computation is applied independently to many pieces of data.\nL22: Comments:  | This version corrects a confusing typographical error in Section 7. We are grateful to Michael Walfish for identifying the error\nL23: Subjects:  | Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Computational Complexity (cs.CC); Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)\nL24: Cite as:  | 【9†arXiv:1304.3812】 [cs.CR]\nL25:    | (or 【10†arXiv:1304.3812v4】 [cs.CR] for this version)\nL26:    | 【11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1304.3812†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Justin Thaler [【12†view email】]\nL30: 【5†[v1]】 Sat, 13 Apr 2013 14:47:09 UTC (776 KB)\nL31: 【13†[v2]】 Thu, 6 Jun 2013 20:30:31 UTC (777 KB)\nL32: 【14†[v3]】 Sat, 27 Jul 2013 18:23:12 UTC (777 KB)\nL33: [v4] Wed, 8 Feb 2017 18:57:37 UTC (777 KB)\nL34: \nL35: Full-text links:\nL36: \nL37: ## Access Paper:\nL38: \nL39: View a PDF of the paper titled Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation, by Justin Thaler\nL40: \nL41:   * 【7†View PDF】\nL42:   * 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL43:   * 【15†TeX Source】\nL44: \nL45: 【16†view license】\nL46: ### Additional Features\nL47: \nL48:   * 【17†Audio Summary】\nL49: \nL50: ### Current browse context:\nL51: \nL52: cs.CR\nL53: \nL54: 【18†< prev】   |   【19†next >】\nL55: \nL56: 【20†new】 | 【21†recent】 | 【22†2013-04】\nL57: \nL58: Change to browse by:\nL59: \nL60: 【23†cs】\nL61: 【24†cs.CC】\nL62: 【25†cs.DS】\nL63: \nL64: ### References & Citations\nL65: \nL66:   * 【26†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL67:   * 【27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL68:   * 【28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL69: ### 【29†DBLP†dblp.uni-trier.de】 - CS Bibliography\nL70: \nL71: 【30†listing†dblp.uni-trier.de】 | 【31†bibtex†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL72: \nL73: 【32†Justin Thaler†dblp.uni-trier.de】\nL74: \nL75: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL76: \nL77: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL78: \nL79: [Button: ×]\nL80: \nL81: loading...\nL82: \nL83: Data provided by:\nL84: \nL85: ### Bookmark\nL86: \nL87: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL88: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL89: \nL90: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL91: \nL92: Bibliographic Explorer (【33†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL93: \n